/* ============================================================================
   amicable-pair-search.epher -- pairs where each sums to the other

   Two numbers are amicable when the proper divisor sum of each is the
   other: ps(220) = 284 and ps(284) = 220. The script computes proper
   divisor sums with the classroom pair method (epher has no divisor-sum
   builtin), checks the classic pair, finds the smallest amicable pair
   below 1000 with a packed while loop, and counts how many pairs lie
   wholly below 1000: just one, 220 and 284. Amicable numbers were known
   to the Pythagoreans (c. 500 BC); the sigma identity is that sigma(a)
   and sigma(b) both equal a + b for an amicable pair.

   Source: Hardy and Wright, "An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers",
   ch. 16, section 16.8 (amicable numbers). Results are exact; the
   recursion depth limits the def to n of a few thousand, plenty here.

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/number-theory/divisors/amicable-pair-search.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\number-theory\divisors\amicable-pair-search.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/number-theory/divisors/amicable-pair-search.epher
   In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

   There is no knob: the bound 1000 is the point of the script.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the proper-divisor-sum def ------------------------------------------------------
// pairdiv(n, i) adds each divisor of n with its partner n / i (a square
// adds its root once), so pairdiv(n, 1) is sigma(n); ps subtracts n.
def pairdiv(n, i) = if i * i > n then 0 else if mod(n, i) == 0 then (if i * i == n then i + pairdiv(n, i + 1) else i + n / i + pairdiv(n, i + 1)) else pairdiv(n, i + 1)
def sigma(n) = pairdiv(n, 1)
def ps(n) = sigma(n) - n

// ---- the classic pair ----------------------------------------------------------------
print("proper divisor sum of 220:", ps(220))    // 1 + ... + 110 = 284
print("proper divisor sum of 284:", ps(284))    // 220 back
print("sigma(220):", sigma(220))                // 504 = 220 + 284, as it must
print("sigma(284):", sigma(284))                // the same 504

// ---- find the smallest pair below 1000 -----------------------------------------------
// v walks a upward until a is the smaller member of an amicable pair:
// its proper sum b lies above it and below 1000, and ps(b) returns a.
v = {2}
while not (ps(v[1]) > v[1] and ps(v[1]) < 1000 and ps(ps(v[1])) == v[1]) do v = {v[1] + 1}
print("smallest amicable pair:", v[1], "and", ps(v[1]))

// ---- how many pairs lie below 1000 -----------------------------------------------------
// The same test for every a from 2 to 999, counted in one packed loop.
v = {2, 0}
while v[1] < 1000 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if ps(v[1]) > v[1] and ps(v[1]) < 1000 and ps(ps(v[1])) == v[1] then 1 else 0)}
print("amicable pairs below 1000:", v[2])       // only 220 and 284
/* ---- expected output ----
= proper divisor sum of 220: 284
= proper divisor sum of 284: 220
= sigma(220): 504
= sigma(284): 504
= {2}
= smallest amicable pair: 220 and 284
= {2, 0}
= amicable pairs below 1000: 1
*/
